insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('53bae41c-9b40-5fc1-9fa4-1bdcdbb430d1', 'wg9-argument-writing-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Student Interpreters at Larchmont High$q$, $q$(1) At Larchmont High School, families speak more than twenty languages at home, including Spanish, Vietnamese, Amharic and Tagalog. (2) Yet the school has only two paid interpreters for its family conference nights, and many parents wait an hour or leave without speaking to a teacher. (3) The school should train bilingual juniors and seniors to serve as volunteer interpreters on conference nights. (4) Last year, the front office recorded that 140 families signed in for conferences but left before meeting any teacher. (5) Many of those families had requested an interpreter on their sign-up forms.

(6) Some people believe that students should not interpret because conversations about grades can be private. (7) This concern is serious. (8) For example, student interpreters would help only families who choose to accept them, and they would never interpret for a classmate's family or repeat what they hear. (9) The paid interpreters would still handle any meeting that a family or teacher considers sensitive.

(10) The program would help the volunteers as well. (11) Volunteers would earn service hours and practice a skill that many careers value, from medicine to business. (12) A similar program at Westbury High trained thirty students in its first year, and the share of families who met with at least one teacher rose from 61 percent to 83 percent. (13) My friend speaks three languages. (14) A trained team of student interpreters would help more families take part in their children's education while giving students meaningful experience.$q$, 'draft')
