   $q$Sentence (13) gives a vague reason. Which revision would make the conclusion follow most clearly from the argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Clearly, the board must protect the orchard, because old things are always worth keeping.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"The museum should also restore the old barn and add a gift shop that sells cider.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"introduces-new-topic"},{"text":"Because the orchard holds rare trees that scientists want, the parking problem has a simple fix, and the trees could draw new visitors, the board should keep it.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Clearly, the board must protect the orchard, and anyone who disagrees does not understand history.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dismissive-tone"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Because the orchard holds rare trees that scientists want, the parking problem has a simple fix, and the trees could draw new visitors, the board should keep it.$q$, $q$The correct revision replaces "history matters" with the essay's three actual reasons from paragraphs one, two and three. Saying old things are always worth keeping is a broad rule the essay never argues.$q$, 'draft', 'c02e7959-c79e-51f0-b3d8-c97d30e469cc')
